WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.004
A SOC 2 Type II report evaluates both control design and operating
effectiveness over a defined review period.

2
00:00:08.004 --> 00:00:14.229
The review period may vary, but the key idea is consistent operation over
time.

3
00:00:14.229 --> 00:00:20.010
A Type II report asks not only whether the control was designed suitably,

4
00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:24.457
but whether it actually operated as described during the period.

5
00:00:24.457 --> 00:00:28.903
This is why Type II usually requires more evidence discipline.

6
00:00:28.903 --> 00:00:36.907
A control owner cannot rely on one screenshot from the report date if the
control is supposed to

7
00:00:36.907 --> 00:00:41.354
run weekly, monthly, quarterly, or whenever a triggering event occurs.

8
00:00:41.354 --> 00:00:48.913
The evidence needs to show that the control operated at the required
frequency, that exceptions were identified,

9
00:00:48.913 --> 00:00:53.804
and that remediation or follow-up happened when needed. Consider access
reviews.

10
00:00:53.804 --> 00:01:00.474
For Type II, the team may need evidence that reviews occurred during the
review period,

11
00:01:00.474 --> 00:01:07.144
that the right population was reviewed, that reviewers had appropriate
responsibility, that exceptions were tracked,

12
00:01:07.144 --> 00:01:09.812
and that access changes were completed.

13
00:01:09.812 --> 00:01:16.927
For change management, evidence may include tickets, approvals, testing
notes, deployment records, and emergency change follow-up.

14
00:01:16.927 --> 00:01:22.708
For monitoring, evidence may include alert configurations, review records,
escalations, and incident handling.

15
00:01:22.708 --> 00:01:30.712
Type II also tests whether the organization can sustain the program when
the audit is not the only

16
00:01:30.712 --> 00:01:37.826
thing happening. People join and leave. Services change. Vendors are
added. Incidents occur. Systems are patched.

17
00:01:37.826 --> 00:01:41.384
Controls need to operate through normal business conditions.

18
00:01:41.384 --> 00:01:48.943
That is why Type II often provides stronger assurance to customers: it
shows operation, not only design.

19
00:01:48.943 --> 00:01:55.613
Operating effectiveness does not mean there can never be an exception.
Real programs have exceptions.

20
00:01:55.613 --> 00:02:01.838
The important question is whether exceptions are identified, documented,
evaluated, remediated, and explained honestly.

21
00:02:01.838 --> 00:02:08.953
Trying to hide control failures or backfill evidence dishonestly
undermines the entire purpose of SOC 2.

22
00:02:08.953 --> 00:02:12.955
A sustainable program treats exceptions as signals for improvement.

23
00:02:12.955 --> 00:02:20.514
For teams preparing for Type II, the best approach is to build evidence
collection into normal workflows.

24
00:02:20.514 --> 00:02:27.629
Ticketing, access review tools, HR processes, monitoring systems, incident
response records, backup tests, vendor review workflows,

25
00:02:27.629 --> 00:02:31.631
and policy acknowledgments should produce evidence as work happens.

26
00:02:31.631 --> 00:02:38.301
If evidence collection depends on a frantic scramble at the end of the
review period,

27
00:02:38.301 --> 00:02:42.747
the program is harder to trust and harder to operate.
